## form_sibling_watches fires zero times in all benchmark runs

**Where it belongs:** `dreth/v30_integration.py` (on_signature_change, Case 3).

**The gap:**
The form sibling horizon shortening (Part 3 of predictive failure) only triggers in
Case 3 of `on_signature_change` — a genuine operator mismatch unbind (e.g., FIRST →
HIGH). All observed unbinds in benchmark runs are Case 1 (parent binding changed,
same operator) or "rebinding." Case 3 hasn't been observed, so `form_sibling_watches`
is always 0.

Case 1 unbinds also represent structural change worth propagating to siblings — if a
var's parent structure changed significantly enough to break its form binding, that's
evidence the causal graph shifted in a way that could affect other instances. Consider
extending sibling notification to include Case 1 unbinds where `parents_changed` is
True and the binding mismatch is large (e.g., parent set changed by more than one
variable).

Add first-class contextual tie/frontier tracking to VarNethra: when fit_var returns tied or near-tied candidates, do not discard them as noise or force a single winner unless probes actually separate them. Store the candidate constellation with scores, margin, context/regime key, collapse signature, and any known separating probes. Treat this as “operationally equivalent under current context,” not as trass: trass means target-irrelevant, while a tie means multiple explanations currently predict/control the same. Use the frontier to preserve state across tied churn, choose future probes that split the constellation when needed, and archive dormant alternatives when one candidate becomes decisively better.